#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --quiet --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.11"
# dependencies = ["pyyaml>=6.0", "ruamel.yaml>=0.18"]
# ///
"""Switch Cold Turkey Blocker between work modes defined in one config note.

    focus                     # menu: pick a mode, timer or session (list when piped)
    focus list                # the modes, timers and sessions, and what runs now
    focus research            # stop the other 'mode: …' blocks, start this one
    focus off                 # stop every unlocked 'mode: …' block
    focus break [MIN] [--emergency]  # pause the modes; --emergency skips the hour's wait
    focus status              # what runs now (mode, break, only, timer), every block by kind, drift
    focus sync                # add missing sites to existing mode blocks through the CLI
    focus export [PATH]       # write a .ctbbl file for Blocks → Import blocks…
    focus screenzen [--dry-run]  # write ScreenZen's groups from the same note
    focus add travel westjet.com  # add to a group in the note, then sync both apps
    focus pull [--dry-run]    # copy sites and apps added inside Cold Turkey into the note
    focus deep [MINUTES]      # a timer: block its groups on top of any mode, locked
    focus only [GROUP…] [no:NAME…] [MINUTES]  # pick what to allow; the rest gets a pause screen
    focus CAPPED [MINUTES]    # a capped mode: at most its minutes, then back to the mode before
    focus CAPPED --day [MIN]  # one longer stretch from its daily budget
    focus research 90 --commit  # keep the mode's block locked for 90 minutes
    focus allow SITE…         # during a commitment: allow sites once the lock runs out
    focus end                 # during a commitment: end it once the lock runs out
    focus alfred [QUERY]      # Alfred script-filter JSON (config/alfred/workflows/focus)
    focus install-agent       # launchd job, every minute: rolls the lock, ends what is due
    focus uninstall-agent     # remove that job (deploy.sh --no-focus)

Every site and app is defined once, in a named group. Modes and the always-on
blocklists only name groups:

    groups:       {papers: {sites: [arxiv.org]}, comms: {apps: [Telegram]}}
    always_allow: [papers]                     # allowed in every mode
    modes:        {research: {allow: [papers], block: [comms]}}
    blocklists:   {screenzen: {"🚫 distractions": {block: [comms]}},
                   cold_turkey: {"always": {block: [comms], allow: [papers]}}}

Two layers. ScreenZen gives friction and follows the mode: focus owns one allow
group per mode, '✅ focus: <mode>', plus '✅ focus: shared' for no mode, each
holding the shared list plus that mode's sites minus what ScreenZen's own 🚫
groups block; the no-mode group holds every mode's sites, so a mode only
tightens it. Exactly one is on, so ScreenZen shows the mode; every other site
gets a pause screen. A switch or 'off' turns the right one on. Its 🚫 groups are
written from `blocklists.screenzen`, and all other ✅ groups are off. ScreenZen has
no CLI, so each write saves a
backup to $FOCUS_BACKUPS (default ~/scratch/blocker-backups), closes it,
writes its preferences with `defaults`, reads them back and reopens it.
ScreenZen matches by substring, so an allow pattern is cut at its first '*' (it
then allows at least what Cold Turkey does) and a block pattern loses its '*'s.

Cold Turkey blocks hard, one mode at a time. Each mode is a block named
'mode: <name>' whose website list is '*.*' and whose exceptions are the sites of
`always_allow` plus the mode's `allow` groups; it blocks the apps of its `block`
groups, since Cold Turkey cannot allowlist apps. Only one mode runs at a time:
two running allowlist blocks would allow only the sites that are in both.
A mode with `kind: blocklist` is the reverse: its website list is the sites of its
`block` groups, so the rest of the web opens, and ScreenZen switches every ✅ group
off while it runs, as in an open window. Its `allow` groups still feed '✅ focus:
shared'. Starting one, even from no mode, loosens, so an agent waits.
`blocklists.cold_turkey` describes the always-on blocks; `status` reports their
drift but nothing writes them, because their delay locks stop the CLI. A site a
mode allows but an always-on block bans stays banned, and `status` flags it.
`strong_lock_blocks` names blocks that should carry a random-text lock: `status`
warns, in yellow, for each one that has no lock or only a delay lock.

'focus only' is a session like Cold Turkey Micromanager that can be bypassed. With no
groups it opens a search over the groups and every installed app in no group, where
SPACE or TAB ticks several and the header lists them (in Alfred, 'only' piles one
pick per ENTER into the query and the top row starts them); a word
that is not a group name finds the one group holding that app or site ('chatgpt' finds
ai-chat), else the one installed app it names ('zotero'). ScreenZen does all the
work: its '✅ focus: only' group holds the note's `only.keep` groups plus the picks, and
a '🚫 focus: only' group holds every installed app except those, so anything else gets
a pause screen that can be passed. `only.default` lists picks a new session starts
with (groups, or installed apps in no group): the picker shows them ticked, so SPACE
unticks one; named picks bring them along, and a word 'no:NAME' leaves one out
('focus only ai-chat no:spotify'). During a session 'focus only no:NAME' takes a pick
away. A session uses no Cold Turkey block: Cold Turkey can
only be passed with a command, and the point is to reach something forgotten without
leaving the work. So it stops the running mode, and only the always-on blocks stay
hard. It lives in a state file, not in `modes`. When the time is up focus sends a
notification and starts the mode it replaced. While a session runs, 'focus only' adds
groups and 'focus only 20' sets the end to 20 minutes from now; 'focus only end' or any
mode ends it. The section `only:` in the note switches all this on.

A capped mode (`capped_modes: {<mode>: {minutes: 30, day_minutes: 180, fallback:
<mode>}}`) runs for at most `minutes` a stretch, then switches back to the mode that
ran before it, or to `fallback` if none did, and sends a notification. A waiter and
the once-a-minute launchd job end it, so it ends after a restart too. Renewing at
once is allowed, but every start of that mode after the first of the local day asks
for a one-line reason at the terminal (and refuses without one), appended with the
time and the command to ~/.local/state/focus/commit-log.jsonl. '--day' runs one
longer stretch from the daily budget `day_minutes`, asking the reason once; a day
stretch left early gives back its unused minutes. A capped mode is never brought
back without its cap: a break or only session returns to the mode before it.

A default mode (`default_mode: {mode: <mode>, start: "09:00", until: "12:00", days:
[mon, tue, wed, thu, fri]}`, those being the defaults) is started by the launchd job
once a day, on its first tick between `start` and `until` on those days, if nothing
runs: no mode, break, only session, capped stretch or commitment, and not in
off_hours. That first tick marks the day either way, so a mode stopped by hand is not
started again until the next day. Only the job does this, never a command.

A scheduled mode (`scheduled_mode: {mode: admin, start: "21:30", until: "22:00",
days: [...]}`, every day by default) must be a capped mode. The launchd job starts it
once a day on its first tick in that span, as a stretch that ends at `until` and then
leaves no mode running, not the mode before it: admin, then the evening social hour
(Yulong, 2026-11-18). It asks no reason and takes no daily start. A committed block or
an only session skips it for the day.

An agent (CLAUDECODE=1) loosens only after a delay (`agent_loosen_delay_minutes`,
default 10): stopping a mode, switching modes, a sync that allows new sites, or
a ScreenZen write that allows more or blocks less. The first call queues that
exact request and exits 3; the same call after the delay runs it. Starting a
mode from none, and any change that only tightens, run at once. People are
not delayed by this, with one exception: a committed block delays a person too
(2026-11-18).

'focus <mode> [MINUTES] --commit' (default 60 min) starts the mode and keeps its
block locked until the end time with a rolling lock: the launchd tick (and every
focus command, first) keeps the lock about `commit_wait_minutes` (default 5, at
least 2) ahead with '-start <block> -lock N',
and starts and locks the block again if it finds it stopped before the end with
nothing pending (a missed tick, sleep, a locked screen), logging that. It refuses
unless 'launchctl print' shows the job loaded and running this focus with this
note (a plist on disk proves nothing), and it leaves a detached keeper that runs
the same check every 30 s until the end. While it runs, 'break', 'off', 'only' and
starting another mode refuse; 'focus deep' still runs. Only two commands change it,
and only at a terminal: 'focus allow SITE…' and 'focus end'. Each shows a random
text of `commit_text_chars` (default 300) characters to type back exactly, asks for
a one-line reason (logged to ~/.local/state/focus/commit-log.jsonl), then stops
extending the lock and leaves a waiter. When Cold Turkey's lock runs out and it
stops the block, the waiter applies the request within a few seconds: for allow,
it adds the sites to the note group 'added-<mode>' (named in the mode's allow
list), adds them to the block as exceptions, then starts and locks the block again;
for end, it stops the block and clears the commitment, and ScreenZen follows as for
'off'. Only sites can be allowed this way, not apps. At the end time the last lock
runs out and the mode stays on, unlocked. The state is ~/.local/state/focus/
commit.json. Accepted limitation: unloading the launchd job and stopping the keeper
(or restarting with the job unloaded), or deleting the state file, lets the lock
lapse within one wait.

The config is a Markdown note with one fenced ```yaml block, kept in the vault so
personal entries stay out of this public repo. Override its path with
--config or $FOCUS_CONFIG.

Cold Turkey's CLI can start and stop blocks and add sites, but cannot create a
block, add an app or remove anything. `export` covers those: it writes the mode
blocks in Cold Turkey's own export format, which Import blocks… reads. `status`
reads Cold Turkey's settings database (an obfuscated JSON blob) and never writes it.
"""

